James inquired about a benefit-cost ratio (BCR) of 14 from a report and sought an example to illustrate its significance. I explained that a BCR of 14 indicates that every dollar invested yields $14 in benefits. I provided specific examples of projects achieving this BCR, such as public health campaigns (e.g., smoking cessation programs), environmental restoration (e.g., wetland restoration), and infrastructure improvements (e.g., public transportation expansions). Each example underscored the substantial societal benefits that can exceed costs, reinforcing their viability in sustainability efforts.
